{
  "summary": "Escapes embedded newline characters as hex sequences within the logging subsystem while stripping a single conventional trailing newline prior to escaping and re-appending it. Introduces a `util::SplitLines` callback utility and updates multiline logging sites—including exception handlers, UI message boxes, and wallet diagnostics—to output each line as an individual log entry. Adds unit tests for the line-splitter and log escaping, along with functional tests verifying that rejected RPC method calls and wallet names cannot inject newlines into the log.",
  "problem": "Untrusted inputs such as rejected RPC method names or wallet names can contain newlines that pass through logging unescaped. Restricted RPC users or callers can exploit this to forge arbitrary log lines in debug.log, potentially misleading node operators or triggering false positives in automated monitoring tools.",
  "evidence": [
    "The PR description provides a reproducible bash script demonstrating that a restricted RPC user can pass a newline-delimited string to inject a forged `ERROR: ConnectTip: ConnectBlock ... failed` line into `debug.log`."
  ],
